{"id":"hemeonc-pharmacology","system":"Hematology & Oncology","subject":"Pharmacology","pages":[440,448],"rounds":[{"t":"Tumor lysis syndrome and heparin","q":[{"s":"A patient with Burkitt lymphoma starts chemotherapy and develops weakness, arrhythmia and acute kidney injury. Which electrolyte pattern is expected?","o":["↓ K+, ↓ phosphate, ↑ Ca2+","↑ Ca2+ and ↓ uric acid","↓ Na+ only","Normal electrolytes with high LDH only","↑ K+, ↑ phosphate, ↓ Ca2+, ↑ uric acid"],"e":["Wrong. Opposite.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. Tumor lysis syndrome (\"BreaK the CUP\")."],"a":4,"p":"440","k":"Tumor lysis syndrome"},{"s":"Why does hypocalcemia occur in tumor lysis syndrome?","o":["PTH is suppressed by tumor cells","Calcium moves into tumor cells","Calcium is lost in urine","Released phosphate binds and sequesters calcium","Vitamin D is destroyed"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not the mechanism.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"440","k":"Tumor lysis syndrome"},{"s":"Which drug converts uric acid to allantoin to prevent kidney injury in tumor lysis?","o":["Probenecid","Rasburicase","Allopurinol","Colchicine","Febuxostat"],"e":["Wrong. Uricosuric; not used here.","Correct. Recombinant uricase.","Wrong. Inhibits xanthine oxidase.","Wrong. Gout flares.","Wrong. Inhibits xanthine oxidase."],"a":1,"p":"440","k":"Tumor lysis syndrome"},{"s":"Heparin produces anticoagulation mainly by inhibiting which factors through antithrombin?","o":["Factors II, VII, IX, X synthesis","Factors IIa (thrombin) and Xa","Factor XIII","Platelet COX","Factors V and VIII"],"e":["Wrong. Warfarin.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Aspirin.","Wrong. Protein C."],"a":1,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"Which anticoagulant is preferred in pregnancy?","o":["Warfarin","Dabigatran","Low-molecular-weight heparin","Apixaban","Rivaroxaban"],"e":["Wrong. Teratogenic.","Wrong. Not preferred.","Correct. Does not cross the placenta.","Wrong. Not preferred.","Wrong. Not preferred."],"a":2,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"Unfractionated heparin is monitored with which test?","o":["PT/INR","Bleeding time","Platelet count only","PTT","Thrombin time only"],"e":["Wrong. Warfarin.","Wrong. Platelets.","Wrong. Monitors HIT, not effect.","Correct.","Wrong. Not standard."],"a":3,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"A patient on heparin bleeds heavily. Which agent reverses it?","o":["Andexanet alfa","Tranexamic acid","Idarucizumab","Protamine sulfate","Vitamin K"],"e":["Wrong. Xa inhibitors.","Wrong. Antifibrinolytic.","Wrong. Dabigatran.","Correct. A positively charged peptide binding negatively charged heparin.","Wrong. Warfarin."],"a":3,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"Seven days after starting unfractionated heparin, a patient's platelet count falls from 250,000 to 70,000/mm³ and he develops a DVT. What is the mechanism?","o":["Nonimmune platelet aggregation in the first 2 days","Splenic sequestration from portal hypertension","IgG antibodies against heparin–platelet factor 4 complexes activate platelets","Dilution from fluids","Marrow suppression by heparin"],"e":["Wrong. HIT type 1.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. HIT type 2.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"What is the appropriate management of HIT type 2?","o":["Give protamine and observe","Stop heparin and start argatroban (or fondaparinux)","Continue heparin and transfuse platelets","Switch to enoxaparin","Start warfarin immediately alone"],"e":["Wrong. Not sufficient.","Correct.","Wrong. Dangerous.","Wrong. Cross-reacts.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"HIT type 1 is best described as:","o":["A mild, transient, nonimmune drop within 2 days, not clinically significant","Causing platelets <20,000/mm³","Occurring 5–10 days after heparin","An IgG-mediated, thrombotic disorder","Requiring immediate argatroban"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Type 2.","Wrong. Type 2.","Wrong. Not needed."],"a":0,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"Which is a long-term adverse effect of heparin?","o":["Pulmonary fibrosis","Osteoporosis","Ototoxicity","Hepatotoxicity","Gynecomastia"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. Also type 4 RTA (hyperkalemia).","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not the classic answer.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"A patient on heparin develops hyperkalemia with a normal anion gap acidosis. Which renal effect is responsible?","o":["Type 2 RTA","Type 1 RTA","SIADH","Nephrogenic DI","Type 4 RTA"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. Heparin reduces aldosterone."],"a":4,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"Compared with unfractionated heparin, LMWHs such as enoxaparin:","o":["Act mainly on factor Xa and have a longer half-life","Are fully reversed by protamine","Act mainly on thrombin","Are preferred in renal failure","Have a shorter half-life"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Not easily reversible.","Wrong. Mainly Xa.","Wrong. Renally cleared; use caution.","Wrong. Longer."],"a":0,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"Fondaparinux acts on:","o":["Thrombin only","Both IIa and Xa equally","Platelet P2Y12","Vitamin K epoxide reductase","Factor Xa only"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Warfarin.","Correct. Does not interact with PF4, so it is safe in HIT."],"a":4,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"A patient with severe renal insufficiency needs anticoagulation for a new PE. Which agent is preferred?","o":["Dabigatran","Enoxaparin","Dalteparin","Unfractionated heparin","Fondaparinux"],"e":["Wrong. Renally cleared.","Wrong. Renally cleared.","Wrong. LMWH.","Correct.","Wrong. Renally cleared."],"a":3,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"Which is a clinical use of heparin?","o":["Treatment of HIT","Treatment of hemophilia","Immediate anticoagulation for acute coronary syndrome or PE","Reversal of warfarin","Long-term stroke prevention as an oral agent"],"e":["Wrong. It causes HIT.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not oral."],"a":2,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"Which cancers most often cause tumor lysis syndrome?","o":["Lymphomas and leukemias","Thyroid cancer","Meningioma","Basal cell carcinoma","Slow-growing prostate cancer"],"e":["Correct. Fast-growing, chemosensitive tumors.","Wrong. Not typical.","Wrong. Not typical.","Wrong. Not typical.","Wrong. Not typical."],"a":0,"p":"440","k":"Tumor lysis syndrome"},{"s":"Which measure helps prevent tumor lysis syndrome before chemotherapy?","o":["Aggressive hydration and allopurinol","Phosphate supplements","Potassium supplements","Fluid restriction","Calcium infusion routinely"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Worsens it.","Wrong. Worsens hyperkalemia.","Wrong. Opposite.","Wrong. Not routine."],"a":0,"p":"440","k":"Tumor lysis syndrome"},{"s":"Why does fondaparinux not cause HIT type 2?","o":["It is a protein","It has a short half-life","It does not interact with platelet factor 4","It is given orally","It inhibits thrombin directly"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"},{"s":"Which heparin type carries the highest risk of HIT type 2?","o":["Fondaparinux","Dalteparin","Argatroban","Enoxaparin","Unfractionated heparin"],"e":["Wrong. No PF4 interaction.","Wrong. Lower risk.","Wrong. Not a heparin.","Wrong. Lower risk.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"440","k":"Heparin"}]},{"t":"Warfarin, direct oral anticoagulants, and reversal","q":[{"s":"Warfarin inhibits which enzyme?","o":["Vitamin K epoxide reductase","Factor Xa","Thrombin","Cyclooxygenase","γ-glutamyl carboxylase directly"],"e":["Correct. Prevents γ-carboxylation of II, VII, IX, X, C and S.","Wrong. Xa inhibitors.","Wrong. Direct thrombin inhibitors.","Wrong. Aspirin.","Wrong. Not the target."],"a":0,"p":"441","k":"Warfarin"},{"s":"Polymorphisms in which gene affect warfarin dosing?","o":["HLA-B*5701","G6PD","CYP2D6","VKORC1","TPMT"],"e":["Wrong. Abacavir.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not the classic answer.","Correct. Also CYP2C9 metabolism.","Wrong. Thiopurines."],"a":3,"p":"441","k":"Warfarin"},{"s":"Which mnemonic links warfarin to its lab test?","o":["\"Don't Trast HER\"","\"Taxes stabilize society\"","\"When heparin is BAD\"","\"Play Table Tennis inside\"","\"The ex-PresidenT went to war(farin)\""],"e":["Wrong. Trastuzumab.","Wrong. Taxanes.","Wrong. Direct thrombin inhibitors.","Wrong. PTT/intrinsic.","Correct. Extrinsic pathway, PT."],"a":4,"p":"441","k":"Warfarin"},{"s":"Why is warfarin contraindicated in pregnancy?","o":["It causes HIT","It crosses the placenta and is teratogenic","It causes maternal hypertension","It is ineffective in pregnancy","It is cleared too quickly"],"e":["Wrong. Heparin.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"441","k":"Warfarin"},{"s":"Why is there an initial hypercoagulable state when warfarin is started?","o":["Factor VIII rises","Antithrombin falls","Platelets are activated","Protein C has a shorter half-life than factors II and X, so it falls first","Fibrinogen increases"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"441","k":"Warfarin"},{"s":"Why is heparin used as a \"bridge\" when starting warfarin?","o":["It is required for warfarin metabolism","It prevents HIT","It reverses warfarin","It speeds warfarin absorption","It anticoagulates during the transient hypercoagulable state and lowers skin necrosis risk"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"441","k":"Warfarin"},{"s":"Warfarin is metabolized mainly by which enzyme, making it prone to drug interactions?","o":["Xanthine oxidase","Alcohol dehydrogenase","CYP2C9","CYP3A4 only","TPMT"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not the listed enzyme.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"441","k":"Warfarin"},{"s":"Which is an indication for warfarin rather than a DOAC?","o":["HIT","A mechanical heart valve","Pregnancy","Atrial fibrillation without valve disease","Uncomplicated DVT"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Correct. DOACs are not used with mechanical valves.","Wrong. Warfarin is contraindicated.","Wrong. DOACs are suitable.","Wrong. DOACs are suitable."],"a":1,"p":"441","k":"Warfarin"},{"s":"Compared with heparin, warfarin acts:","o":["In the liver, with slow onset and a duration of days","Monitored by PTT","In the blood, with rapid onset","Parenterally, for hours","Without crossing the placenta"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Heparin.","Wrong. Heparin.","Wrong. Heparin.","Wrong. Warfarin crosses."],"a":0,"p":"441","k":"Heparin vs warfarin"},{"s":"Which direct thrombin inhibitor is used in patients with HIT?","o":["Apixaban","Rivaroxaban","Argatroban","Warfarin","Enoxaparin"],"e":["Wrong. Xa inhibitor.","Wrong. Xa inhibitor.","Correct. Also bivalirudin; \"when heparin is BAD.\"","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. LMWH."],"a":2,"p":"441","k":"Direct coagulation factor inhibitors"},{"s":"Apixaban, edoxaban and rivaroxaban act by:","o":["Directly inhibiting factor Xa","Blocking P2Y12","Activating antithrombin","Directly inhibiting thrombin","Inhibiting vitamin K epoxide reductase"],"e":["Correct. \"Ban\" factor Xa.","Wrong. Clopidogrel.","Wrong. Heparin.","Wrong. Dabigatran.","Wrong. Warfarin."],"a":0,"p":"441","k":"Direct coagulation factor inhibitors"},{"s":"Which statement about direct oral anticoagulants is correct?","o":["They are preferred in pregnancy","They should not be used in patients with mechanical heart valves","They are reversed by protamine","They require INR monitoring","They act through antithrombin"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Usually no monitoring.","Wrong. Direct action."],"a":1,"p":"441","k":"Direct coagulation factor inhibitors"},{"s":"A patient on dabigatran has a major bleed. Which reversal agent is used?","o":["Idarucizumab","Protamine","Desmopressin","Andexanet alfa","Vitamin K"],"e":["Correct. Monoclonal antibody Fab fragments.","Wrong. Heparin.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Xa inhibitors.","Wrong. Warfarin."],"a":0,"p":"442","k":"Anticoagulation reversal"},{"s":"Andexanet alfa reverses which drugs?","o":["Warfarin","Dabigatran","Heparin","Apixaban and rivaroxaban","Clopidogrel"],"e":["Wrong. Vitamin K, PCC.","Wrong. Idarucizumab.","Wrong. Protamine.","Correct. Recombinant inactive factor Xa decoy.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"442","k":"Anticoagulation reversal"},{"s":"A patient on warfarin has an INR of 9 with intracranial hemorrhage. Which is the best immediate treatment?","o":["Vitamin K alone","Platelet transfusion","Protamine","Idarucizumab","PCC (or FFP) plus vitamin K"],"e":["Wrong. Too slow.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Heparin.","Wrong. Dabigatran.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"442","k":"Anticoagulation reversal"},{"s":"Why does protamine bind heparin?","o":["Protamine is a vitamin K analog","Protamine is positively charged and heparin is negatively charged","Protamine activates factor X","Protamine degrades antithrombin","Protamine is an antibody to heparin"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"442","k":"Anticoagulation reversal"},{"s":"Warfarin increases which coagulation test?","o":["Platelet count","Bleeding time","D-dimer","PT/INR","PTT alone"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Platelets.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Heparin."],"a":3,"p":"441","k":"Warfarin"},{"s":"Warfarin skin necrosis is caused by:","o":["Allergic vasculitis","HIT antibodies","Small-vessel microthrombosis during early protein C depletion","Infection","Calcium deposition"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Heparin.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Calciphylaxis."],"a":2,"p":"441","k":"Warfarin"},{"s":"Which is a clinical use of direct Xa inhibitors?","o":["Reversal of heparin","Mechanical heart valves","DVT/PE treatment and stroke prophylaxis in atrial fibrillation","Treatment of hemophilia","Treatment of TTP"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not used.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"441","k":"Direct coagulation factor inhibitors"},{"s":"Which anticoagulant is given orally?","o":["Enoxaparin","Unfractionated heparin","Bivalirudin","Argatroban","Warfarin"],"e":["Wrong. SC.","Wrong. IV/SC.","Wrong. IV.","Wrong. IV.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"441","k":"Heparin vs warfarin"}]},{"t":"Antiplatelets, thrombolytics, and cancer drug targets","q":[{"s":"Aspirin irreversibly inhibits which enzyme?","o":["GpIIb/IIIa","P2Y12","Plasminogen","Phosphodiesterase","Cyclooxygenase"],"e":["Wrong. Eptifibatide.","Wrong. Clopidogrel.","Wrong. Thrombolytics.","Wrong. Cilostazol.","Correct. Reduces TXA2."],"a":4,"p":"442","k":"Antiplatelets"},{"s":"Which is an adverse effect of aspirin?","o":["Hemorrhagic cystitis","Tinnitus","Ototoxicity with nephrotoxicity","Gynecomastia","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"e":["Wrong. Cyclophosphamide.","Correct. Also gastric ulcers, renal injury and Reye syndrome in children.","Wrong. Cisplatin.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Bleomycin."],"a":1,"p":"442","k":"Antiplatelets"},{"s":"Clopidogrel, prasugrel and ticagrelor act by:","o":["Blocking the ADP (P2Y12) receptor","Activating antithrombin","Blocking GpIIb/IIIa directly","Inhibiting phosphodiesterase","Blocking COX"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Heparin.","Wrong. Eptifibatide.","Wrong. Cilostazol.","Wrong. Aspirin."],"a":0,"p":"442","k":"Antiplatelets"},{"s":"A man with leg pain when walking that resolves with rest is started on a phosphodiesterase inhibitor. Which drug?","o":["Alteplase","Cilostazol","Abciximab","Clopidogrel","Aspirin"],"e":["Wrong. Thrombolytic.","Correct. For intermittent claudication; raises platelet cAMP.","Wrong. GpIIb/IIIa.","Wrong. P2Y12.","Wrong. COX."],"a":1,"p":"442","k":"Antiplatelets"},{"s":"Eptifibatide and tirofiban are used during percutaneous coronary intervention. Which adverse effect is typical?","o":["Neuropathy","Ototoxicity","Hypercoagulability","Hepatotoxicity","Bleeding and thrombocytopenia"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"442","k":"Antiplatelets"},{"s":"Dipyridamole is used in which clinical setting?","o":["Warfarin reversal","HIT treatment","Cardiac stress testing and stroke prevention","Hemophilia","Acute MI thrombolysis"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Argatroban.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. tPA."],"a":2,"p":"442","k":"Antiplatelets"},{"s":"Thrombolytics such as alteplase cause which laboratory changes?","o":["↓ PT","↑ fibrinogen","No lab changes","↓ platelet count only","↑ PT and ↑ PTT with no change in platelet count"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Platelets unchanged.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"442","k":"Thrombolytics"},{"s":"A patient with an acute ischemic stroke 2 hours ago is being considered for alteplase. Which history is a contraindication?","o":["Atrial fibrillation","Hyperlipidemia","Previous intracranial hemorrhage","Age over 60","Diabetes"],"e":["Wrong. Not a contraindication.","Wrong. Not a contraindication.","Correct. Also active bleeding, recent surgery, bleeding diathesis and severe hypertension.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not a contraindication."],"a":2,"p":"442","k":"Thrombolytics"},{"s":"Which agents provide nonspecific reversal of thrombolytic-related bleeding?","o":["Andexanet alfa","Aminocaproic acid, tranexamic acid, cryoprecipitate and platelets","Desmopressin only","Protamine and vitamin K","Idarucizumab"],"e":["Wrong. Xa inhibitors.","Correct.","Wrong. Not listed.","Wrong. Not the listed agents.","Wrong. Dabigatran."],"a":1,"p":"442","k":"Thrombolytics"},{"s":"Which anticancer drug class acts in the M phase of the cell cycle?","o":["Microtubule inhibitors (taxanes, vinca alkaloids)","Antimetabolites","Platinum compounds","Alkylating agents","Topoisomerase inhibitors"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. S phase.","Wrong. Nonspecific.","Wrong. Nonspecific.","Wrong. S/G2."],"a":0,"p":"443","k":"Cancer therapy—cell cycle"},{"s":"Which drug class is cell cycle–independent?","o":["Antimetabolites","Bleomycin","Taxanes","Alkylating agents","Vinca alkaloids"],"e":["Wrong. S phase (except cladribine).","Wrong. G2.","Wrong. M phase.","Correct. Also platinum compounds, dactinomycin.","Wrong. M phase."],"a":3,"p":"443","k":"Cancer therapy—cell cycle"},{"s":"Methotrexate and 5-FU both decrease synthesis of:","o":["Microtubules","Purines de novo","Topoisomerase","Thymidine","RNA only"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Thiopurines.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":3,"p":"443","k":"Cancer therapy—targets"},{"s":"Hydroxyurea inhibits which enzyme?","o":["HGPRT","Dihydrofolate reductase","Topoisomerase II","Ribonucleotide reductase","Thymidylate synthase"],"e":["Wrong. Activates 6-MP.","Wrong. Methotrexate.","Wrong. Etoposide.","Correct.","Wrong. 5-FU."],"a":3,"p":"443","k":"Cancer therapy—targets"},{"s":"Irinotecan and topotecan inhibit:","o":["Topoisomerase I","Ribonucleotide reductase","Topoisomerase II","Microtubule assembly","DNA polymerase"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Hydroxyurea.","Wrong. Etoposide.","Wrong. Vinca.","Wrong. Cytarabine."],"a":0,"p":"443","k":"Cancer therapy—targets"},{"s":"Which drug class intercalates into DNA?","o":["Topoisomerase I inhibitors","Antimetabolites","Anthracyclines and dactinomycin","Proteasome inhibitors","Taxanes"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Microtubules."],"a":2,"p":"443","k":"Cancer therapy—targets"},{"s":"Ado-trastuzumab emtansine (T-DM1) is an example of:","o":["A small-molecule tyrosine kinase inhibitor","A vaccine","An alkylating agent","An immune checkpoint inhibitor","An antibody-drug conjugate for HER2-positive breast cancer"],"e":["Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct."],"a":4,"p":"443","k":"Antibody-drug conjugates"},{"s":"What is the advantage of antibody-drug conjugates?","o":["They do not require antigen expression","Targeted delivery to tumor cells increases efficacy and reduces toxicity","They act only on DNA","They are given orally","They avoid all toxicity"],"e":["Wrong. They do.","Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"443","k":"Antibody-drug conjugates"},{"s":"Which proteins modulate the G1 restriction point?","o":["Rb and p53","PD-1 and CTLA-4","BRAF and MEK","BCR and ABL","HER2 and EGFR"],"e":["Correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":0,"p":"443","k":"Cancer therapy—cell cycle"},{"s":"Bleomycin damages DNA by:","o":["Cross-linking strands","Inhibiting thymidylate synthase","Blocking microtubules","Generating free radicals that cause strand breaks","Inhibiting topoisomerase I"],"e":["Wrong. Alkylating agents.","Wrong. 5-FU.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Irinotecan."],"a":3,"p":"443","k":"Cancer therapy—targets"},{"s":"Thiopurines act by decreasing:","o":["Thymidine synthesis","Microtubule formation","De novo purine synthesis","Ribonucleotide reductase","Topoisomerase activity"],"e":["Wrong. MTX/5-FU.","Wrong. Vinca.","Correct.","Wrong. Hydroxyurea.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":2,"p":"443","k":"Cancer therapy—targets"}]},{"t":"Antitumor antibiotics and antimetabolites","q":[{"s":"A man treated for testicular cancer develops progressive dyspnea and dry cough. CT shows interstitial fibrosis. Which drug is responsible?","o":["Cisplatin","Vincristine","Bleomycin","Etoposide","Doxorubicin"],"e":["Wrong. Nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity.","Wrong. Neuropathy.","Correct. Also skin hyperpigmentation.","Wrong. Myelosuppression.","Wrong. Cardiomyopathy."],"a":2,"p":"444","k":"Antitumor antibiotics"},{"s":"Dactinomycin is used to treat:","o":["Hairy cell leukemia","Wilms tumor, Ewing sarcoma and rhabdomyosarcoma","Hodgkin lymphoma only","Testicular cancer only","Colon cancer"],"e":["Wrong. Cladribine.","Correct.","Wrong. Bleomycin.","Wrong. Not the classic answer.","Wrong. 5-FU."],"a":1,"p":"444","k":"Antitumor antibiotics"},{"s":"A woman who received doxorubicin for breast cancer develops dyspnea and edema. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with low ejection fraction. Which drug could have prevented this?","o":["Dexrazoxane","Rasburicase","Amifostine","Mesna","Leucovorin"],"e":["Correct. An iron chelator that protects against anthracycline cardiotoxicity.","Wrong. Tumor lysis.","Wrong. Nephrotoxicity.","Wrong. Hemorrhagic cystitis.","Wrong. Methotrexate."],"a":0,"p":"444","k":"Antitumor antibiotics"},{"s":"Anthracyclines act by which mechanisms?","o":["Inhibiting microtubule polymerization","Generating free radicals, intercalating DNA and inhibiting topoisomerase II","Inhibiting thymidylate synthase","Cross-linking DNA","Blocking BCR-ABL"],"e":["Wrong. Vinca.","Correct.","Wrong. 5-FU.","Wrong. Alkylating agents.","Wrong. Imatinib."],"a":1,"p":"444","k":"Antitumor antibiotics"},{"s":"Anthracycline cardiotoxicity is typically:","o":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy, often irreversible","Prevented by mesna","Reversible within days","Pericarditis only"],"e":["Wrong. Dilated.","Correct.","Wrong. Dexrazoxane.","Wrong. Often irreversible.","Wrong. Not correct."],"a":1,"p":"444","k":"Antitumor antibiotics"},{"s":"Azathioprine is converted to 6-mercaptopurine, which is activated by which enzyme?","o":["Xanthine oxidase","TPMT","ADA","HGPRT","Dihydrofolate reductase"],"e":["Wrong. Inactivates 6-MP.","Wrong. Inactivates 6-MP.","Wrong. Not correct.","Correct.","Wrong. Methotrexate."],"a":3,"p":"444","k":"Antimetabolites"},{"s":"A patient on azathioprine for Crohn disease develops severe pancytopenia aft